## 12. Regularization & Training Techniques

### 12.1 Regularization
| Technique | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| **Dropout** | Randomly zero out activations during training |
| **DropPath/Stochastic Depth** | Randomly drop entire layers (residual blocks) |
| **Weight Decay (L2)** | Penalize large weights: $L_{total} = L + \lambda\|w\|^2$ |
| **Label Smoothing** | Replace hard labels [0,1] with soft labels [0.1/K, 0.9+0.1/K] |
| **Data Augmentation** | Acts as implicit regularization |
| **Early Stopping** | Stop training when validation loss stops improving |
| **Batch Normalization** | Has mild regularization effect |

### 12.2 Learning Rate Scheduling
| Schedule | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| **Step Decay** | Multiply LR by factor every N epochs |
| **Cosine Annealing** | LR follows cosine curve from max to min |
| **Warmup + Cosine** | Start with very low LR, linearly increase, then cosine decay |
| **OneCycleLR** | Cycle from low → high → low LR during training |
| **ReduceOnPlateau** | Reduce LR when validation loss plateaus |

```python
# Cosine Annealing with Warmup
scheduler = torch.optim.lr_scheduler.CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts(optimizer, T_0=10)

# OneCycleLR
scheduler = torch.optim.lr_scheduler.OneCycleLR(optimizer, max_lr=1e-3, 
                                                  total_steps=epochs*steps_per_epoch)
```

### 12.3 Optimizers for CV
| Optimizer | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| **SGD + Momentum** | Classic; often best for CNNs with proper LR schedule |
| **Adam** | Adaptive learning rate; good default; works well for Transformers |
| **AdamW** | Adam with decoupled weight decay; preferred for modern training |
| **LAMB** | Large batch optimization |
| **SAM** | Sharpness-Aware Minimization; finds flatter minima for better generalization |

### 12.4 Mixed Precision Training
Use FP16 (half precision) for most computations, FP32 for critical operations. 2x speedup, less memory.

```python
from torch.cuda.amp import autocast, GradScaler

scaler = GradScaler()
for images, labels in loader:
    with autocast():
        outputs = model(images)
        loss = criterion(outputs, labels)
    scaler.scale(loss).backward()
    scaler.step(optimizer)
    scaler.update()
```

