## 13. Pose Estimation

### The Task
Detect body keypoints (joints) in an image/video.

### 13.1 Types
| Type | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **Single-person** | Estimate keypoints for one person |
| **Multi-person top-down** | Detect people first (bounding boxes), then estimate pose per person |
| **Multi-person bottom-up** | Detect all keypoints first, then group them into people |

### 13.2 Key Models
| Model | Approach |
|-------|----------|
| **OpenPose** | Bottom-up; uses Part Affinity Fields (PAFs) |
| **HRNet** | High-Resolution Networks; maintains high resolution throughout |
| **MediaPipe Pose** | Google's real-time pose on mobile |
| **RTMPose** | Real-time multi-person pose estimation |
| **ViTPose** | Vision Transformer for pose estimation |

### 13.3 Keypoint Formats
- **COCO format**: 17 keypoints (nose, eyes, ears, shoulders, elbows, wrists, hips, knees, ankles).
- Each keypoint: `(x, y, visibility)` where visibility = 0 (not labeled), 1 (labeled but occluded), 2 (visible).

### 13.4 Evaluation
- **OKS (Object Keypoint Similarity)**: Like IoU but for keypoints.
  $$OKS = \frac{\sum_i \exp(-d_i^2 / 2s^2k_i^2) \cdot \delta(v_i > 0)}{\sum_i \delta(v_i > 0)}$$
  where $d_i$ = distance, $s$ = object scale, $k_i$ = per-keypoint constant.
- **AP (Average Precision)**: Computed over OKS thresholds (like mAP for detection).

